1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,680
When you do modeling your input data can come from two sources

2
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:07,430
Either it's already in the data warehouse

3
00:00:07,430 --> 00:00:11,081
because it has been integrated by an integration tool

4
00:00:11,081 --> 00:00:17,500
like 5 Fran or AirBike or any custom ETL tool or ETL process

5
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:21,860
or it's a smaller dataset that you want to send

6
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:25,556
from your laptop directly to the data warehouse

7
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:27,840
The latter is called a seed

8
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:31,830
and if you have the data already in the data warehouse

9
00:00:31,830 --> 00:00:34,876
that will be called a source in dbt

10
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:38,800
And dbt provides tools for defining seeds and sources

11
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:42,974
and also to make sure that your sources are up to date

12
00:00:42,974 --> 00:00:47,033
and get alerting on stale outdated sources

13
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:48,998
So let's see how this works in practice
